# ::tok Personally, I think it's acceptable. First, the price is cheap, and the more than 700 pages are divided into two books, which are better to read as a paperback; second, it is different from the complete works of Michelangelo, the paintings account for the main body, and Leonardo Da Vinci's black and white manuscript accounts for most of it. For non-art professional readers with a limited budget, this edition is the most cost-effective for appreciating paintings and manuscripts.
